{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 27, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a 2010 american biographical drama film directed by david fincher and written by jesse eisenberg, aron sorkin, and stewart ross. the film is based on the true story of the founding of the social networking site facebook and the resulting lawsuits between its creators and harvard college.\n\nthe film stars jesse eisenberg as mark zuckerberg, a sophomore at harvard who creates a website called \"facemash\" that allows users to compare the photos of two students and vote on which one is more attractive. the site becomes popular, but also sparks controversy and is eventually shut down by the university.\n\nmark and his roommates, eduardo saverin (andrew garfield) and andrew mccollum (josh zuckerman), decide to create a new website called \"facebook\" that will allow harvard students to create profiles and connect with each other. the site quickly gains popularity and soon expands to other colleges and universities.\n\nas facebook's popularity grows, so does the tension between mark and his friends. eduardo, who is from a wealthy family in brazil, is increasingly frustrated with the fact that he is not getting the recognition he feels he deserves for his contributions to the site. andrew mccollum, who was originally supposed to be the primary developer of the site, becomes disillusioned with the direction the site is taking and eventually leaves.\n\nmeanwhile, a sophomore named erin (rooney mara) becomes interested in mark and they start a relationship. however, erin is also friends with a group of students, including a girl named cambridge (alice gainer) and her boyfriend, a guy named sean (donald logue), who are not happy about mark's increasing popularity and the fact that he is spending more and more time on facebook and less time with his friends.\n\nthe film also explores the lawsuit that was filed against mark and his friends by the winklevoss twins, cambridge and tyler (armie hammer and josh pence), who claim that they came up with the idea for facebook first and that mark stole it from them.\n\nthe film features a strong performance from jesse eisenberg as mark zuckerberg. he perfectly captures the complexities and nuances of the character, from his awkwardness and introversion to his ambition and drive. the film also features a great supporting cast, including andrew garfield, justin timberlake, and armie hammer.\n\nthe film's direction by david fincher is also noteworthy. he uses a lot of close-ups and rapid cuts to create a sense of energy and tension, and he also uses a lot of dark colors and shadows to create a sense of unease and foreboding.\n\nthe film's script by jesse eisenberg and aron sorkin is also excellent. it is witty and engaging, and it perfectly captures the tone and atmosphere of the story. the script also explores some interesting themes, including the nature of friendship and the consequences of ambition.\n\noverall, the social network is a great film that is well worth watching. it is a fascinating and thought-provoking story that explores some complex themes and features a strong cast and excellent direction. if you are a fan of biographical dramas or are interested in the story of facebook, then this is definitely a film you should check out.\n\nrating: 9/10\n\nnote: this review is based on the original theatrical cut of the film.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.045232724}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -0.0016868184}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -7.6293654e-06}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.18040486}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.00012385078}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.32515162}, {"token": "201", "logprob": -2.9086643e-05}, {"token": "0", "logprob": -3.933899e-06}, {"token": " american", "logprob": -0.3342006}, {"token": " bi", "logprob": -0.0031711787}, {"token": "ographical", "logprob": -0.00043990472}, {"token": " drama", "logprob":